FUNDAMENTAL ANALYSIS TOOLS
Benjamin Graham's Timeless Investment
Principles
By Updated November 21, 2024BRIAN DOLAN
Reviewed by MARGUERITA CHENG
Fact checked by VIKKI VELASQUEZ
Warren Buffett is widely considered to be one of the greatest investors of all
time, but if you were to ask him who he thinks is the greatest investor, he
probably would mention one man: his former teacher, Benjamin Graham.
Graham was an investor and investing mentor who is generally considered the
father of security analysis and value investing.
His ideas and methods of investing are well-documented in his books "Security
Analysis" (1934) and "The Intelligent Investor" (1949), which are two of the most
famous investing texts ever written. These texts are often considered requisite
reading material for any investor, but they aren't easy reads.
In this article, we'll condense Graham's main investing principles and give you a
head start on understanding his winning philosophy.
KEY TAKEAWAYS
Benjamin Graham is considered a legend in the investing field, having
authored two key books on the subject, "Security Analysis" (1934), and
"The Intelligent Investor" (1949).
